## DnG-Crafts/K2-RFID

<https://github.com/DnG-Crafts/K2-RFID>

**Spoolworks would not exist without this project.** The Creality tag format, the two AES keys, the
material-database layout and the printer paths were all worked out there first, across a Windows
app, an Android app and Arduino firmware for three boards. Spoolworks began as the macOS port of
it, contributed back as that project's PR #51.

Files from it that are still here verbatim:

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `reference/arduino-aes/AES.cpp`, `AES.h` | ESP32 firmware AES. `Tools/aes-reference-check.sh` compiles it natively to generate golden test vectors, which is what proves this app's crypto is byte-identical to the firmware that programs real tags |
| `reference/colors.db` | The Windows app's colour database (see below for its contents) |

The Swift source is a reverse-engineered reimplementation rather than a translation. `SPEC/` and the
doc comments cite upstream files and line numbers throughout — those citations point at
DnG-Crafts/K2-RFID, not at anything in this repository, and they are the evidence trail for how the
tag format was established.

Upstream publishes no licence file of its own.

## meodai/color-names

<https://github.com/meodai/color-names> — MIT licensed.

The 31,861-entry colour-name dataset inside `reference/colors.db`, compiled by
`Tools/build-color-table.sh` into `Sources/SpoolworksCore/Resources/colors.bin`. This is what turns
a filament's `RRGGBB` into "Sakura" or "Galaxy Black" on screen.

## filamentcolors.xyz

<https://filamentcolors.xyz> — MIT licensed, by Joe Kaufeld
(<https://github.com/itsthejoker/filamentcolors.xyz>). Credit is requested for reuse; this is it.

`Sources/SpoolworksCore/Resources/filament-swatches.json` — 2,258 filament colours across 150
manufacturers, used for the colour picker's manufacturer lists. **The hex values are colorimeter
readings of physical printed swatches**, published alongside their LAB values, rather than
marketing images. Cached locally as the project asks; `reference/filamentcolors-swatches.json`
keeps the fetch with its database version so it can be validated against theirs later.

Worth stating plainly: a measurement of *printed* filament is a shade off the raw spool and further
off whatever a factory tag encodes. These are for **choosing** a colour, not for deciding what a
Creality tag says.

## Creality

Material databases and printer files produced by Creality, read from devices the owner of this
repository owns:

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `Sources/SpoolworksCore/Resources/{k1,k2,hi}.json` | The bundled material catalogues the app seeds from |
| `reference/seeds/{k1,k2,hi}.json` | Their provenance copies |
| `reference/printer-dumps/*.json` | Files pulled off a K2 Plus. `material_box_info.json` doubles as a test fixture — it is what proves the serial-collision problem in the README is real rather than hypothetical |

## OrcaSlicer filament library

<https://github.com/OrcaSlicer/OrcaSlicer> — **AGPL-3.0**.

`Sources/SpoolworksCore/Resources/vendor-k2.json` — 194 third-party filament records across eight
brands, assembled by `Tools/build-vendor-catalogue.py`. Bambu Lab, Elegoo, Polymaker's consumer
line, Overture and SUNLU come from `resources/profiles/OrcaFilamentLibrary/filament`, where the
filament makers contribute one profile per product. Anycubic, Flashforge and Prusament come from
their own printer-vendor folders, which hold one profile *per product per printer per nozzle* —
128, 421 and 59 files for 27, 46 and 8 products — collapsed to the 0.4 mm variant, since that is
the nozzle every record here declares.

**What is taken and what is not.** Eighteen keys per record at most, every one a measurable property
of the plastic: density, the nozzle and heated-bed temperatures, glass-transition temperature, flow
ratio, maximum volumetric speed, the soluble and support flags, required nozzle hardness, and
whether the material wants the air filtered. Product names and the list of which products exist.
Nothing else —
no G-code, no retraction or fan tuning, no pressure advance, no purge volumes, and none of the
Bambu ecosystem's interchangeable build-plate temperatures, which describe a plate rather than a
plastic. Those describe the
extruder a profile was written for, not the filament, and each record instead inherits them from
the Creality generic record for its polymer (named in `sourceTemplate`). Every record also carries
`sourceProfile`, the resolved inheritance chain it was read from.

**A value is taken only where the maker stated it.** These profiles inherit from shared polymer
bases, and a base supplies a default for every key whether or not anyone measured it. Importing
those defaults as though they were the maker's figures is how `Anycubic ASA` ends up claiming PLA's
1.24 g/cm³. So each value is traced to the file that set it, and one that came from a base or a
`Generic` profile is left behind — the record then inherits Creality's figure for that polymer
instead. Where two of a maker's own printer profiles state the same value differently (55 of the
194 records disagree about something that changes a print), the 0.4 mm variant wins and the
alternatives are written onto the record as `sourceConflicts`, so the choice can be audited rather
than taken on trust.

**The licence question, stated plainly.** OrcaSlicer is AGPL-3.0 and its `resources/profiles`
directory carries no separate licence, so the library is AGPL-3.0 by default. This repository is
MIT. The position taken here is that a density and a melt temperature are measurements of a
physical product rather than creative expression, that a re-expression into Creality's schema under
our own identifiers is not a copy of OrcaSlicer's compilation, and that no expressive content
(the G-code) travels. That is a considered position and not a legal opinion. Anyone redistributing
this who reads it differently has two clean alternatives: drop `vendor-k2.json` from the bundle and
run `Tools/build-vendor-catalogue.py` locally, which distributes nothing, or ask the filament makers
directly — several already publish presets under their own terms.

